          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. The Specs That Actually Predict Satisfaction
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Horizontal and vertical operation.</strong> The most important feature on any splitter handling wood over about 16 inches. Rolling a heavy round into position beats lifting it, and this is the difference between splitting big wood and avoiding it. Check how the conversion works in person — some designs require pulling pins and fighting the beam, and those get used in horizontal mode forever.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Two-stage pump.</strong> Almost all decent splitters have one, and it is what gives a fast return stroke. Verify it rather than assuming, because a slow return affects every single split you make.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Beam construction.</strong> The full beam design, where the cylinder sits above rather than beside the beam, resists flex better under load. Flex is what eventually cracks welds, and beam mass is the clearest visible indicator of how a splitter will age.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Log cradles.</strong> Wings on either side of the beam that catch split halves instead of letting them fall to the ground. This sounds trivial and is not: without cradles you bend over to retrieve every piece, which over a cord of wood is hundreds of repetitions and the reason your back hurts after a day of splitting.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Engine quality.</strong> On a machine that sits unused for nine months a year, starting reliability outranks horsepower. Honda GX engines are the benchmark; Champion and Briggs are dependable; the cheapest generic engines are where value machines save money and where owners spend their patience.
          </p>
