        <!-- SECTION 7 -->
        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Safety: Where People Get Hurt
          </h2>

          <p>
            Log splitters cause a specific and predictable set of injuries, and nearly all of them come from hands near the wedge while the ram is moving. The rule that prevents most of them is simple: position the round, then move both hands clear before touching the control. Never reposition a piece that is already under pressure, and never ask a second person to hold or adjust wood while you operate the lever — two-person operation is exactly how crush injuries happen.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wood under pressure is stored energy. A round that is partially split can release suddenly and throw a piece with considerable force, so eye protection is not optional and standing directly in line with the wedge is a bad habit. Gloves protect against splinters but make it easier to get a hand caught; keep them, and keep your hands off the beam.
          </p>

          <p>
            Set up on firm level ground. A splitter that shifts or sinks under load while you are handling a 150-pound round is a hazard in itself, and in vertical mode the machine's stability is what is keeping that round from rolling onto your foot. Steel-toed boots are genuinely worth wearing for this work.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 8 -->
        <div id="maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Maintenance and Off-Season Storage
          </h2>

          <p>
            Splitters are mechanically simple and fail mostly from neglect during the nine months they sit idle. Check the hydraulic fluid level before each session and change fluid and filter at the interval in the manual, typically every 100 hours or annually — contaminated hydraulic fluid is the most common cause of a pump that loses pressure.
          </p>

          <p>
            The engine is where seasonal storage matters most. Either run the fuel system dry or fill the tank and add stabiliser before the machine sits; the half-full tank of untreated gasoline is what gums a carburetor over a winter and produces the spring ritual of pulling a starter cord at a machine that will not catch. Change the oil at the end of the season rather than the start, so it does not spend the winter sitting on used oil.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bleed the cylinder if the ram starts to feel spongy or hesitant at the end of stroke, and inspect the beam welds once a season with the machine clean — a hairline crack found early is a weld repair, while the same crack found late is a new beam. Neither task takes ten minutes, and together they are most of what separates a splitter that lasts a decade from one that lasts twenty-five years.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep the wedge sharp. A dull wedge needs noticeably more force and is more prone to pushing stringy wood aside rather than cutting it; a few minutes with a file each season restores it. Grease the beam slide, check hose fittings for weeping, and store the machine under cover if you can — sun destroys hydraulic hoses faster than use does.
          </p>
        </div>


