            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best log splitter in 2026?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The Champion Power Equipment 27-Ton is our overall pick: enough force for anything a homeowner will meet, a 12-second cycle, fast vertical conversion, and reliable starting. The Cub Cadet LS 27 CCHP is better built and worth the premium if you split every year for decades. For a cord or two of seasoned wood split in a garage, the Boss Industrial ES7T20 electric is the smarter tool.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many tons do I need in a log splitter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For seasoned oak, maple and ash up to 20 inches, 20 to 22 tons handles the overwhelming majority of homeowner firewood. Green hardwood and rounds over 20 inches want 25 to 27 tons. Stringy species such as elm and sweetgum, and knotted crotch pieces, are the only wood that genuinely needs 27 tons or more — and some of it resists any amount of force.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are electric log splitters any good?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, within their limits. A 7-ton electric splitter handles seasoned straight-grained hardwood up to about 20 inches, runs indoors with no fumes, needs no fuel or carburetor maintenance, and starts instantly after months of sitting. It will stall on knotty or green hardwood, and the power cord rules out woodlot work. For one to two cords of seasoned wood a year it is the better tool.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it cheaper to rent or buy a log splitter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Renting runs $80 to $110 a day and a pair of people can split around three cords in one long day, so purely on cost, buying makes sense at roughly two to three cords a year. The stronger argument for owning is scheduling: renting means splitting everything in one exhausting session in whatever weather arrives, while owning lets you do an hour at a time.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is a good cycle time for a log splitter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Twelve seconds or under keeps a two-person crew — one splitting, one stacking — working continuously without waiting. Between 13 and 15 seconds is acceptable for solo work. Above 15 seconds the wait becomes noticeable on every round and a long day feels considerably longer. Cycle time affects your experience far more than an extra five tons of force.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a splitter that goes vertical?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If you split rounds over about 16 inches, yes. A green 24-inch oak round can weigh 200 pounds, and repeatedly lifting that onto a horizontal beam is the most common way a firewood season ends in a back injury. Vertical mode lets you roll the round into position and split it on the ground. Check that the conversion is quick — awkward designs never get used.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long do log splitters last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Fifteen to twenty-five years is normal with basic care, because the hydraulics are simple and lightly stressed relative to their design. What actually fails is the engine on a machine stored with untreated fuel, and hoses left in the sun. Change hydraulic fluid annually, stabilise or drain the fuel each autumn, and keep it under cover, and the splitter will outlast the chainsaw by a wide margin.
              </p>
            </div>

