        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Log Splitter Buying Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            These machines last decades, so a purchase made on the wrong specification is a mistake you live with for a very long time.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Tonnage Instead of Cycle Time</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Thirty tons with an 18-second cycle processes less wood per hour than 22 tons with an 11-second cycle, because the extra force sits unused on nearly every round while the slow return taxes all of them. Settle tonnage against your worst wood, then compare cycle times.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping Vertical Operation</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Horizontal-only machines are cheaper and fine for small rounds. For anything over about 16 inches, lifting the round is the hardest and most dangerous part of the job, and a machine that converts to vertical eliminates it entirely. This is the feature owners most often wish they had paid for.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring Log Cradles</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Without wings to catch the split halves, every piece drops to the ground and you bend to pick it up — hundreds of times per cord. Cradles cost little as an accessory and come fitted on better machines, and they are the difference between being tired and being sore.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Storing It With Untreated Fuel</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The most common log splitter failure is not hydraulic; it is a carburetor gummed by gasoline that sat for nine months. Either run the fuel system dry or fill and stabilise the tank before the machine goes away for the year, and change the oil in autumn rather than spring.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Operating With Two People on One Machine</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                One person holding or adjusting the wood while another works the lever is precisely how crush injuries occur. One operator, both hands clear before the ram moves, and never reposition a round that is already under pressure. If a piece is stuck, retract fully and start again.
              </p>
            </div>
